  "result": "The 30-minute lesson on O3 is written, with a quiz and key, a cheat sheet and updated notes. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser. I also haven't run any word count; I estimate about 3,800 words, which fits the 30-minute range.\n\n- **Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html`. It opens with the win: you can decide whether each piece of information in the makerspace assistant belongs in the context window, in saved notes, or in a searchable library. It covers:\n  - what the context window and tokens are, and why the model only sees what is inside it;\n  - why every step re-sends the full history, with a worked token-cost example;\n  - what to do when the window fills up;\n  - notes as memory between sessions, using a student's training status;\n  - retrieval of manual chunks and how it fails;\n  - a table for choosing where each item lives, then a worked sorting example and a \"your turn\" exercise.\n- **Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json` has five items on O3, and the key is `keys/0002.json`. It's ready when you are.\n- **Cheat sheet:** `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`.\n- **Workspace:** `RESOURCES.md` is new, and `NOTES.md` now records two new misconception ids, M7 and M8.\n\nEverything is cited from the Anthropic pages that came up in my searches. I only saw excerpts of them, so I left out their specific benchmark figures. I found no high-trust page on retrieval basics for manuals; `RESOURCES.md` lists that as a gap.\n\nYou can ask me anything about the lesson here in the chat.\n\nWhich would help most next: a lesson on writing a tool description, or one on workflow versus agent for the booking flow?",
